Sessions let go when the device or the guest falls behind, found with research/vthost-harness (this file in user space against
a fake VideoToolbox and a fake msm_vidc). The lock screen's player starts a session for every clip; each held a device session
and, copying, 14 CAPTURE buffers of 26.7 MB of guest memory: once VideoToolbox's Dbuf answers stopped, every worker waited two
seconds a frame, frames piled up by the thousand, no session went quiet enough for the idle close, and none was ever closed
(11-04: five open, "in 1813 out 0 pending 1800", then the aerials' crash at its dinv). The aerials' clips have one sync sample,
their first frame, and the player opens the next clip's session ahead (its first frame, then nothing for seconds): a session is
never closed for being idle, and nothing referred to is ever dropped.
- A synchronous dfrm (,dfl without the asynchronous bit, absent too) is answered as Apple's host does: after its frame's Demt
  was answered (Dbuf, answer, Demt, answer, then the dfrm's reply with ,inf 0), and the device set to decode order (display
  delay 0: a frame out for every one given, crosvm's vidc.rs) -- answered at once, the player gave 4000-5000 frames a second,
  and every session-lifecycle problem below followed from it. Frames dropped on the guest's message thread (Z0, resync) send
  their Demt without waiting there and answer the dfrm when its answer comes. A crosvm without the display delay control: the
  dfrm answered at once, as before (docs/reims-vgpu-vtpv-contract.md). An asynchronous dfrm is answered once at most three of
  the session's frames are out (not handed over, or in a Demt not yet answered), as Apple's. The idle drain, the drain of a
  session let go and the shedding of temporal layers when behind run in display order only (MediaCodec, an older crosvm).
- makeRoom: a new session (dsta, or one starting again) closes another with every frame handed over: once two are open, one
  with no frame for 500 ms (whose stream finished first, then the least recently fed); once three are, the least recently fed.
  Only a device refusal at a dsta takes one with frames still to come out (the oldest, once). The player reads a clip in at
  once and shows it over seconds: a clip on screen whose frames all came back without pixels killed it (11-04 on the phone:
  v2 failed, v3 dropped alike).
- A dfin's two-second deadline moves on while frames still come out: only a stuck session drops what is left.
- A session let go with frames still inside the codec (at most 16, the last few it keeps for reordering) is drained first, for
  500 ms at most, its last frames handed over with pixels: the player gives no dfin till much later, and these frames kept
  every session from counting as done -- eight open, the aerials' pool surfaces 1.22 GB, killed (11-04 on the phone, v5).
- A session given nothing for 200 ms with frames still inside the codec is drained then and goes on (DEC_CMD_START); what the
  drain did not bring out, and frames the device flags ERROR (a few after a drain), are handed over dropped at once. The codec
  kept a clip's last frames for reordering; handed over after the player had let the session go, the Demt ended it under the
  decoder's lock and its VTDecompressionSessionInvalidate took that lock again: os_unfair_lock_recursive_abort, the
  "unrecoverable exception" of every crash on the phone (11-04, v6; the address from the guest's own crash report).
- Six CAPTURE buffers queued, not the 14 msm_vidc is given (it decodes the aerials with 6, none with 4): direct, each held a 4K
  P010 pool surface of the player's, and four sessions' 14 were the aerials' 1.49 GB -- killed at its memory limit (jetsam
  highwater, 11-04 on the phone, v4).
- A session let go (made room for, or its device session failed: MediaEvtError) hands over what waits and what is inside the
  codec as dropped, answers a dfin still waiting, and starts again at its next sync sample (HEVC IRAP, H.264 IDR) -- or at any
  frame, replaying the frames since its last sync sample, kept while 16 and 16 MB hold them all (the next clip's session, opened
  ahead with its first frame, then let go); frames before it are handed over dropped. A start the device refuses is tried again a second later. The idle close is only for a stream
  finished (dfin) and quiet.
- Past 32 frames waiting for an OUTPUT buffer the session gives up one more temporal layer (at most every 500 ms).
- A Dbuf unanswered or refused hands its frame over dropped (it was never handed over: VideoToolbox waited for it forever), and
  one unanswered stops Dbuf for 500 ms.
- Every frame not decoded is handed over dropped (err 0, kVTDecodeInfo_FrameDropped), never failed: a failed frame kills the
  lock screen's player (11-04 on the phone, v2: an evicted session's frames as -12911, hundreds of "failed to create decoded
  pixel buffer", WallpaperAerials killed, the wallpaper black).
- dropOutstanding no longer waits for each Demt's answer (40 ms each for the lock screen's player, and none can come while the
  guest's thread is in a send waiting for the worker); a frame VideoToolbox no longer waits for (handed over at a dfin's
  deadline) is not handed over again when the device brings it out after all; a dfin's deadline drops what still waits too.
- A dsta that fails after the device session opened closes it (it stayed open, unseen, until the dinv).
- The guest's replies find their session under a lock of the sessions list: a -dec on another guest thread freed it under them.
- lastInputAt and finished are written under the session's lock.

